@@ -0,0 +1,22 @@
+# Algorithm-specialization experiments
+
+Each file is a standalone `hc` input. Compile with
+
+    ./.build/debug/hc --emit ir -o /tmp/out.ir <file>.hylo
+
+and read which `ConformanceDeclaration at <file>:<line>` the body of `main` (or the
+generic client) projects: that is the implementation implicit resolution selected.
+
+| file | question | result on `main` | result with specificity ordering |
+|------|----------|------------------|----------------------------------|
+| `e1`  | two overlapping blanket givens (`Bidi` vs `RA`), flat conformances | ambiguous | picks `RA` |
+| `e3`  | same, but the `Bidi` conformance is nested inside the `RA` one | picks `RA` (shorter derivation, by accident) | picks `RA` |
+| `e4`  | specialization seen from a generic caller | lost when the caller is constrained on `Bidi`; kept when constrained on the algorithm trait | unchanged |
+| `e5`  | "refinement-lifting given" carries the specialized implementation | works today, no compiler change | unchanged |
+| `e6`  | the more specific given needs a *deeper* derivation | picks the generic one (BFS stops at the first success) | picks `RA` |
+| `e7`  | two free functions differing only by constraints | ambiguous | still ambiguous (overload resolution not covered) |
+| `e8`  | lifting given vs. a conformance the type declares itself | type's own conformance wins | same (correct: it is more specific) |
+| `e9`  | `<T> => S<T> is P` vs `<T is Q> => T is P` (the `State-of-Generics` example) | picks the former | same (the former really is more specific) |
+| `e11` | two *incomparable* givens | ambiguous | ambiguous (no silent pick) |
+| `e12` | three-level refinement chain | ambiguous | picks the deepest |
+| `lb`  | realistic `lower_bound`, two models, two levels of generic client code | ambiguous | binary search for the random-access model, linear scan for the other |
diff --git a/Notes/specialization-experiments/e1.hylo b/Notes/specialization-experiments/e1.hylo
